#!/usr/bin/env python3
# section: 1
"""hee-cred -- real, minimal GPG-backed credential store.

Real trigger (2026-08-22): "we need to get you all the keys, need a key
store... key/pass store now." Real, scoped answer, three decisions
Spencer made explicitly before any of this was written (not assumed):

  1. Storage: git-tracked, ciphertext only -- .hee/secrets/<account>.gpg,
     same "committed evidence, never plaintext" pattern as everything
     else this session (ratification evidence, seal-secret.sh).
  2. Retrieval: exec-only. `hee cred -pass X` never prints a decrypted
     value -- it decrypts straight into a single child process's
     environment and runs it. This is the same discipline that got a
     plaintext-echo attempt correctly blocked by this session's
     classifier earlier: a decrypted secret must never pass through an
     agent's own visible tool output.
  3. Scope: build for multiple accounts from the start (kiosk, X, etc.),
     not just one -- account names are free text, `user@host`-shaped by
     convention (matches hee-shell's alias style), not hardcoded.

Sibling of fleet-ops/bin/seal-secret.sh (multi-recipient GPG sealing) --
this is the retrieval half that didn't exist yet, built to the same
"never print plaintext" standard.

Real extension (2026-08-22): "-genfrom" -- real, corpus-derived
passphrase generation, folded into hee-cred rather than a new
top-level tool ("picky about top level hee cmds... put it there").
Real trigger: "build cool wordlists... can be for play slip word
thingys" -- real words pulled from real media dialogue (same rg-backed
corpus search as hee-srtscan), joined into a SLIP-39/Diceware-shaped
passphrase. This is generated, not typed, so it doesn't go through the
interactive-terminal prompt -- but the "never printed" invariant still
holds: the generated value flows straight into the same GPG encrypt
call as a typed one, never echoed anywhere.

Real extension (2026-09-12): "-backend age" -- sealing to a machine's
own TPM, for the flippy Debian install. A host that is about to be
installed has no GPG secret key, and the Debian installer has neither a
TPM driver nor a GPG decrypt tool, so its Wi-Fi password and first
account password are sealed with age to an age-plugin-tpm recipient
(age1tag1...) from a live USB, and opened at first boot by the one TPM
that recipient belongs to. Folded in here rather than a new tool, same
reason as -genfrom. The store is the same directory with a different
extension, <account>.age, and -pass/-run choose the backend from the
file that exists -- so every .gpg store and every wrapper keeps working
unchanged. One account never has both: -seal refuses to create the
second, and -pass refuses to guess between them.

Real extension (2026-09-12, later the same day): "-passphrase" and
"-install-keys" -- a person's own identity keys (SSH private key, GPG
secret key) delivered to a freshly bootstrapped host. Spencer: "we will
need to make sure my gpg and ssh keys are on here if we want to have it
work out of the box ... or can we seal them in git? ... maybe it is
first login stuff ... fold this into hee cred". Neither existing backend
fits: GPG needs the GPG key that is being delivered, and a TPM recipient
binds a personal key to one machine (and zeus, the next host, has no
TPM). So the third shape is age's own passphrase mode (scrypt): the
ciphertext is safe in a private repo, opens on any host, and costs one
human moment -- the first login, where -install-keys asks for the
passphrase once, puts the keys where ssh and gpg look, and lays the SOA
anchor (~/.hee/index/_.yaml) with hee-trust. The key files are sealed
as they are, so a key's own passphrase stays as a second layer.

Usage:
  hee-cred -seal <account> -recipients "<gpg-id>[,<gpg-id>...]" [-dir PATH]
  hee-cred -seal <account> -recipients "..." -genfrom TERM [-blocks N] [-root PATH]
  hee-cred -seal <account> -recipients "..." -genkey es256      # prints the public JWKS
  hee-cred -seal <account> -backend age -recipients "<age-recipient>[,...]" [-dir PATH]
  hee-cred -seal <name> -backend age -passphrase [-dir PATH] < ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
  hee-cred -install-keys -dir PATH [-account NAME] [-force]
  hee-cred -pass <account> -exec CMD [ARGS...] [-dir PATH]
  hee-cred -pass <account> -identity FILE -exec CMD [ARGS...]   # an .age credential
  hee-cred -github-app <account> [-registry FILE] [-dir PATH]    # register / check an org's GitHub App
  hee-cred -github-app <account> -key FILE [-force]              # seal its downloaded private key
  hee-cred -run <account> -github-token <grant> -exec CMD [ARGS...]      # CMD gets GH_TOKEN, 1 h, revoked on exit
  hee-cred -reseal <account>|all -add-recipients "<gpg-id>[,...]" [-dir PATH]   # widen who can open, keep who already can
  hee-cred -reseal <account>|all [-dir PATH]        # re-armor a binary .gpg in place: same ciphertext, no decrypt

EXAMPLES
  $ hee cred -github-app github-app-tcos-agents
  $ hee cred -github-app github-app-tcos-agents -key ~/Downloads/tcos-agents.2026-09-13.private-key.pem
  $ hee cred -run github-app-tcos-agents -github-token touchy-claude -exec gh pr list --repo Twin-Cities-Open-Systems/fleet-ops

-seal (typed) reads the secret from an interactive prompt (hidden
input) -- refuses to run at all if stdin isn't a real terminal,
specifically so a secret can never be piped in from a chat/log/script
and end up recorded somewhere it shouldn't.

-seal -genfrom (generated) instead pulls real words out of real media
dialogue matching TERM (ripgrep against /var/local/hee-corpus by
default), joins -blocks of them (default 4) into a real passphrase --
still never printed, same as the typed path.

-seal -genkey es256 (generated) makes a fresh EC P-256 private key with
openssl, seals the PEM exactly like a typed secret, and prints ONLY the
public half as a JWKS document (kid = first 16 hex of sha256 over the
raw point) -- the shape a workload-identity issuer registration wants.
The private key exists nowhere but the sealed file: it is generated in
memory and never written in the clear. Added 2026-09-10 for the lab's
own OIDC issuer (pve agents federating to the Claude Console).

-seal -backend age encrypts with `age` to age recipients instead of GPG
keys -- any age recipient works, and an age-plugin-tpm one binds the
secret to that machine's TPM. Every way of producing the secret (typed,
-genfrom, -genkey) works with either backend.

-seal -backend age -passphrase seals with a passphrase instead of a
recipient (`age -p`, scrypt). age asks for the passphrase itself, on the
controlling terminal, so this needs a terminal even though the plaintext
may arrive on stdin: the one case where -seal reads a redirect, because
an identity key is a multi-line FILE, not a line anyone types. What
arrives must look like a key (an OpenSSH private key or a PGP private
key block) unless -any says otherwise, so a password pasted from a chat
still cannot slip through this path.

-install-keys opens every passphrase-sealed .age file in -dir (age asks
once per file), installs an OpenSSH key under ~/.ssh with its .pub
derived, imports a PGP key block into gpg, creates ~/.hee/secrets, and
runs `hee-trust anchor` when ~/.hee/index/_.yaml is missing. It never
prints key material and never overwrites a differing key without -force.

-pass decrypts and injects the secret into the HEE_CRED_PASS environment
variable of the -exec child process only -- never argv (visible to other
users via `ps`), never printed to stdout/stderr by this tool. An .age
credential needs an identity: -identity FILE, or HEE_CRED_AGE_IDENTITY.
A TPM identity file holds a key wrapped by that TPM -- useless on any
other machine -- and age runs age-plugin-tpm from PATH to use it. A
passphrase-sealed .age file needs no identity: age asks on the terminal.

Real extension (2026-09-13): "-github-app" and "-github-token" -- GitHub
access for the org's agents without a personal token. fleet-ops#387: "no
agent holds a long-lived token. The factory mints a GitHub App installation
token per job, one hour, scoped to that agent's repos and permissions".
Spencer, the same day: "I want to use tokens, not login" and "I own the org,
I create the tokens". A personal access token belongs to the person who made
it; an App belongs to the org. The App's private key is a credential like
any other here, so the lifecycle folds in rather than becoming a new tool.
Everything that is not secret -- the org, the client id, the permission
ceiling, one grant per identity, the keys the private key is sealed to --
lives in the registry (fleet-ops hee/registries/github-apps.registry.v1.yaml,
or $HEE_GITHUB_APPS, or -registry), and the store is the .hee/secrets beside
that registry unless -dir says otherwise, so both flags work from any
directory.

-github-app ACCOUNT walks one App through its life. Not registered yet: it
prints GitHub's registration link, pre-filled from the registry. With -key
FILE, the .pem GitHub downloaded: it checks that the file is an RSA key that
signs and seals it to the registry's recipients (-force replaces an existing
one, for a key rotation). Registered and sealed: it opens the key, asks GitHub what the org's
installation actually holds, and compares that with the registry's ceiling --
more than the ceiling is CRITICAL, less is WARNING.

-reseal ACCOUNT (or `all`, every .gpg in the store) opens a credential HERE,
re-encrypts it to its existing recipients PLUS -add-recipients, checks the
new file opens, and replaces the old one. Real trigger (2026-09-19): a new
host agent (claude@flippy) that the operator wants "used a lot" could open
4 of 17 sealed files, because recipients live in the FILE and every file
predates the key. The manual pipeline in SEALED_CREDENTIALS.md (list-packets,
paste ids, gpg --encrypt, verify) is what this does, without the step where
someone retypes a key id. A file this host cannot open is reported with its
recipients and skipped (WARNING), never guessed at; an .age file is skipped
too, since an age header names no recipients. Nothing is printed but ids.

-reseal with NO -add-recipients normalizes the encoding instead: a .gpg
that is binary OpenPGP (sealed by hand without --armor) is rewritten as
ASCII armor around the SAME ciphertext -- base64 and a CRC24, nothing is
decrypted, no key is needed, so it runs on any host. The new file is
checked with gpg --dearmor to be byte-identical to the old before it
replaces it. Real trigger (2026-09-22): fleet-ops#766 committed the one
binary file among 30 sealed ones, and the inventory service, reading every
sealed file as text, answered 500 on its front page for two days.

-github-token GRANT, with -run or -pass, changes what the child receives: not
the key, but one installation token for that grant -- its repositories
(a regex, a list, or the agent roster's scope.repos) and a subset of the App's
permissions -- as GH_TOKEN (or -as VAR). GitHub's answer is checked against
the request before the child starts, and the token is revoked when the child
exits, so it lives as long as the command, at most one hour.
"""
import argparse
import getpass
import json
import os
import re
import secrets as secrets_mod
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

# library/py lives in this tool's own repo -- derive it from this script's
# own location, resolved through symlinks, never from the repo the tool
# happens to be invoked against.
from pathlib import Path as _Path  # noqa: E402
sys.path.insert(0, str(_Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent.parent / "library" / "py"))
from hee_cli import help_wanted, note_ignored  # noqa: E402

# Relative, so it resolves against the CURRENT directory -- which is a real
# footgun: the same command works in one checkout and reports "no sealed
# credential" in another, with nothing saying why. HEE_CRED_DIR (heerc) lets
# an operator pin one absolute store per machine, so `hee cred -run X` works
# from anywhere. -dir still wins when given.
DEFAULT_DIR = os.environ.get("HEE_CRED_DIR") or ".hee/secrets"
ENV_VAR = "HEE_CRED_PASS"
AGE_IDENTITY_ENV = "HEE_CRED_AGE_IDENTITY"
BACKENDS = ("gpg", "age")
DEFAULT_CORPUS_ROOT = "/var/local/hee-corpus"
WORD_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z']{3,}")
# age -p and its decrypt prompt read the passphrase from the controlling
# terminal, never from stdin. HEE_CRED_TTY exists for the test suite, whose
# fake age never prompts; nothing else should set it.
TTY_PATH = os.environ.get("HEE_CRED_TTY") or "/dev/tty"
KEY_HEADERS = ("-----BEGIN OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY-----", "-----BEGIN PGP PRIVATE KEY BLOCK-----")
ARMOR_BEGIN = b"-----BEGIN AGE ENCRYPTED FILE-----"
AGE_MAGIC = b"age-encryption.org/v1\n"


def die(msg: str, code: int = 2):
    """CRITICAL, exit 2 (Nagios plugin API), the same shape the fleet's
    first-boot and first-login scripts print."""
    print(f"❌ CRITICAL {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(code)


def need_tty(what: str):
    """age prompts for a passphrase on the controlling terminal. Without
    one it fails after the plaintext has already been read, so check first
    and say plainly what is missing."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(TTY_PATH, os.O_RDWR | os.O_NOCTTY)
    except OSError as e:
        die(f"{what} needs a terminal for age's passphrase prompt ({TTY_PATH}: {e.strerror}) -- "
            "run it from an interactive shell, not from a service, a pipe or an agent")
    os.close(fd)


def is_passphrase_sealed(path: Path) -> bool:
    """True when an .age file was sealed with a passphrase (`age -p`): its
    header carries an scrypt stanza. Reads the header only; nothing is
    decrypted, so this is safe on a file this host cannot open."""
    import base64
    try:
        data = path.read_bytes()
    except OSError:
        return False
    if data.startswith(ARMOR_BEGIN):
        lines = [ln.strip() for ln in data.splitlines()[1:] if ln.strip() and not ln.startswith(b"-----")]
        try:
            data = base64.b64decode(b"".join(lines))
        except (ValueError, TypeError):
            return False
    if not data.startswith(AGE_MAGIC):
        return False
    header = data.split(b"\n---", 1)[0]
    return b"\n-> scrypt " in header


def genfrom(term: str, blocks: int, root: str) -> str:
    """Real corpus-derived passphrase: real words pulled from real media
    dialogue matching `term` (same rg-backed search as hee-srtscan), a
    random CSPRNG pick among the real matches -- not the human typing
    anything, but not predictable either."""
    proc = subprocess.run(
        ["/usr/bin/rg", "--json", "-i", "-g", "*.srt", "-g", "*.txt", "--", term, root],
        capture_output=True, text=True,
    )
    if proc.returncode not in (0, 1, 2):
        sys.exit(f"hee-cred: rg failed: {proc.stderr}")

    words = []
    for line in proc.stdout.splitlines():
        try:
            event = json.loads(line)
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            continue
        if event.get("type") != "match":
            continue
        # real edge case: non-UTF8 lines carry "bytes" (base64) instead
        # of "text" -- skip those rather than crash, still real data
        # loss avoided by just not sourcing words from that one line
        text = event["data"]["lines"].get("text")
        if text is None:
            continue
        words += WORD_RE.findall(text)

    words = [w.lower() for w in words if w.isalpha()]
    if len(words) < blocks:
        sys.exit(f"hee-cred: only found {len(words)} real word(s) matching '{term}' -- need at least {blocks}")

    # real CSPRNG pick among real matched words, not the raw match order
    chosen = [secrets_mod.choice(words) for _ in range(blocks)]
    return "-".join(chosen)


def genkey_es256():
    """A fresh P-256 private key (PEM) and its public JWKS. The PEM is the
    secret; the JWKS is the only thing that leaves this function unsealed."""
    import base64, hashlib, json
    key = subprocess.run(["openssl", "ecparam", "-name", "prime256v1", "-genkey", "-noout"], capture_output=True, check=True).stdout
    pub = subprocess.run(["openssl", "ec", "-pubout", "-text", "-noout"], input=key, capture_output=True, check=True).stdout.decode()
    hexs = "".join(l.strip() for l in pub.split("pub:")[1].split("ASN1 OID")[0].splitlines()).replace(":", "")
    raw = bytes.fromhex(hexs)
    if raw[0] != 4 or len(raw) != 65:
        sys.exit("hee-cred: -genkey: unexpected EC point from openssl")
    b64 = lambda b: base64.urlsafe_b64encode(b).rstrip(b"=").decode()
    kid = hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest()[:16]
    jwks = {"keys": [{"kty": "EC", "crv": "P-256", "x": b64(raw[1:33]), "y": b64(raw[33:65]), "kid": kid, "use": "sig", "alg": "ES256"}]}
    return key.decode(), json.dumps(jwks, indent=2)


def encrypt_gpg(secret: str, recipient_list: list, out_path: Path):
    recipient_args = []
    for r in recipient_list:
        recipient_args += ["-r", r]

    # --trust-model always, --batch, --yes: gpg must NEVER prompt here.
    #
    # Measured 2026-09-02. Without --trust-model, an untrusted recipient makes
    # gpg ask "Use this key anyway?" -- and because subprocess hands it a PIPE
    # for stdin, it opens /dev/tty DIRECTLY to ask:
    #
    #   $ printf SECRET | gpg --encrypt --armor -r spencer@kiosk.lab.tcos.us
    #   gpg: 01C9F82A7582A4DC: There is no assurance this key belongs to ...
    #   gpg: cannot open '/dev/tty'
    #
    # /dev/tty is the OPERATOR'S TERMINAL. On a real terminal that prompt
    # appears mid-ceremony and the next thing typed answers it instead of
    # going where it was meant to. Two keys in this fleet's keyring carry
    # trust `-` today, so this is the normal case, not an edge one.
    #
    # Trust checking adds nothing here anyway: -recipients is explicit, so the
    # operator has already named the key. gpg re-asking is asking a question
    # that was answered on the command line.
    #
    # --detach-sign and --verify elsewhere in this toolchain deliberately do
    # NOT get --batch: those genuinely need the terminal for a passphrase.
    # Swept 2026-09-02; this is the only --encrypt site in the org.
    cmd = ["gpg", "--encrypt", "--armor", "--trust-model", "always",
           "--batch", "--yes", *recipient_args, "-o", str(out_path)]
    data = secret if isinstance(secret, bytes) else secret.encode()
    proc = subprocess.run(cmd, input=data, capture_output=True)
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        sys.exit(f"hee-cred: gpg encrypt failed: {proc.stderr.decode(errors='replace')}")


def encrypt_age(secret: str, recipient_list: list, out_path: Path):
    # The ciphertext comes back on stdout and is swapped into place, rather
    # than age writing out_path itself: a re-seal that fails halfway must
    # leave the old credential intact, not a truncated one. Only ciphertext
    # ever touches the disk.
    cmd = ["age", "--encrypt", "--armor"]
    for r in recipient_list:
        cmd += ["-r", r]
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(cmd, input=secret.encode(), capture_output=True)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        sys.exit("hee-cred: -backend age needs `age` on PATH")
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        # A plugin recipient makes age run the matching age-plugin-* even to
        # encrypt, so a missing plugin surfaces here, as age's own message.
        sys.exit(f"hee-cred: age encrypt failed: {proc.stderr.decode(errors='replace')}")
    tmp = out_path.with_name(out_path.name + ".tmp")
    tmp.write_bytes(proc.stdout)
    os.replace(tmp, out_path)


def write_private(out_path: Path, data: bytes):
    """tmp + os.replace, 0600 from the first byte: a failed re-seal leaves
    the old file intact, and nothing is ever readable by the group."""
    tmp = out_path.with_name(out_path.name + ".tmp")
    fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "wb") as f:
        f.write(data)
    os.replace(tmp, out_path)


def encrypt_age_passphrase(secret: str, out_path: Path):
    """`age -p`: age asks for the passphrase (twice) on the terminal; the
    plaintext goes in on stdin and the armored ciphertext comes back on
    stdout, swapped into place like the recipient path."""
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(["age", "--encrypt", "--armor", "-p"],
                              input=secret.encode(), stdout=subprocess.PIPE)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        die("-backend age needs `age` on PATH")
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        die(f"age -p failed (exit {proc.returncode}); nothing written")
    write_private(out_path, proc.stdout)


def seal(account: str, recipients: str, secrets_dir: Path, genfrom_term: str = None, blocks: int = 4, corpus_root: str = DEFAULT_CORPUS_ROOT, genkey: str = None, backend: str = "gpg", passphrase: bool = False, any_kind: bool = False):
    recipient_list = [r.strip() for r in recipients.split(",") if r.strip()]
    if passphrase:
        # -passphrase is an age mode; saying so is the only sane reading of
        # the flag, so it implies the backend rather than demanding it twice.
        backend = "age"
        if recipient_list:
            die("-passphrase seals with a passphrase, not to recipients -- drop -recipients")
        if genfrom_term or genkey:
            die("-passphrase is for identity keys read from stdin (or typed); it does not combine with -genfrom or -genkey")
        need_tty("-seal -passphrase")
    elif not recipient_list:
        what = "age recipient" if backend == "age" else "GPG key id/email"
        sys.exit(f"hee-cred: -recipients required, at least one real {what}")

    # One account, one backend -- checked BEFORE a secret is typed or
    # generated, so nobody enters a secret only to be refused afterwards.
    other = secrets_dir / f"{account}.{'gpg' if backend == 'age' else 'age'}"
    try:
        other_exists = other.exists()
    except OSError:
        other_exists = False
    if other_exists:
        sys.exit(f"hee-cred: {other} already exists -- one account, one backend. "
                 f"Remove it (or seal under another account name) first, so "
                 f"-pass never has to guess which one you meant.")

    jwks = None
    if genkey:
        if genkey.lower() != "es256":
            sys.exit("hee-cred: -genkey supports es256 only")
        secret, jwks = genkey_es256()
        print("hee-cred: generated an EC P-256 signing key (private half sealed, not shown)")
    elif genfrom_term:
        # Generated, not typed -- doesn't need the interactive-terminal
        # guard (nothing is being piped in from outside), but stays
        # unprinted the same as the typed path.
        secret = genfrom(genfrom_term, blocks, corpus_root)
        print(f"hee-cred: generated a {blocks}-word passphrase from real dialogue matching '{genfrom_term}' (not shown)")
    elif passphrase and not sys.stdin.isatty():
        # The one redirect -seal accepts: a key FILE (multi-line, never typed).
        # It must look like a key unless -any says otherwise, so this path
        # cannot become a back door for a password piped in from a chat.
        secret = sys.stdin.read()
        first = secret.lstrip().split("\n", 1)[0].strip() if secret.strip() else ""
        if not secret.strip():
            die("empty stdin, nothing to seal")
        if first not in KEY_HEADERS and not any_kind:
            die("stdin does not start with an OpenSSH private key or a PGP private key block -- "
                "-passphrase seals identity keys; pass -any to seal something else on purpose")
        print(f"hee-cred: read {len(secret.splitlines())} lines from stdin (not shown)")
    else:
        if not sys.stdin.isatty():
            sys.exit(
                "hee-cred: -seal requires an interactive terminal -- refusing "
                "to read a secret from a pipe/redirect/script"
            )
        secret = getpass.getpass(f"hee-cred: enter secret for '{account}' (hidden): ")
        if not secret:
            sys.exit("hee-cred: empty secret, aborting")
        confirm = getpass.getpass("hee-cred: confirm: ")
        if secret != confirm:
            sys.exit("hee-cred: secrets did not match, aborting")
        # Report the CAPTURED length. The double-prompt confirm cannot catch a
        # truncated capture, because both reads truncate identically -- a
        # 69-character key was stored as 1 character, then as 7, and matched
        # itself both times. Length is the only signal the operator can check
        # against what they meant to enter, and it leaks nothing.
        print(f"hee-cred: captured {len(secret)} characters")
        if passphrase and not any_kind and secret.strip() not in KEY_HEADERS:
            die("a typed line is not an identity key -- -passphrase seals key files (redirect one on stdin); "
                "pass -any to seal a typed secret with a passphrase on purpose")

    secrets_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    out_path = secrets_dir / f"{account}.{backend}"
    if passphrase:
        encrypt_age_passphrase(secret, out_path)
        print(f"hee-cred: sealed -> {out_path} (passphrase, scrypt; opens anywhere the passphrase is known)")
    elif backend == "age":
        encrypt_age(secret, recipient_list, out_path)
        print(f"hee-cred: sealed -> {out_path} (recipients: {', '.join(recipient_list)})")
    else:
        encrypt_gpg(secret, recipient_list, out_path)
        print(f"hee-cred: sealed -> {out_path} (recipients: {', '.join(recipient_list)})")
    if jwks:
        print(jwks)   # the PUBLIC half only -- what an issuer registration takes


def derive_var(account: str) -> str:
    """Credential name -> environment variable name.

    plex-token -> PLEX_TOKEN, cf-dns-token -> CF_DNS_TOKEN, do-token ->
    DO_TOKEN. This is not a new convention; it is the one operators were
    already applying by hand in every -exec wrapper, so deriving it removes
    a step rather than adding a rule. -as overrides when a tool wants a name
    that does not follow from the credential's own.
    """
    out = "".join(c if c.isalnum() else "_" for c in account).upper()
    if out and out[0].isdigit():
        out = "_" + out          # an identifier may not start with a digit
    return out


def recipient_keyids(path: Path):
    """Key ids a sealed file is addressed to, for diagnostics only.

    --list-only never decrypts, so this is safe to call on a file this host
    cannot open -- which is exactly when it is needed.
    """
    proc = subprocess.run(
        ["gpg", "--list-packets", "--list-only", str(path)],
        capture_output=True)
    text = (proc.stdout + proc.stderr).decode(errors="replace")
    return re.findall(r"keyid ([0-9A-Fa-f]{8,40})", text)


def keyids_of(recipient: str):
    """Every key id (primary and subkeys) a recipient spec resolves to in
    this keyring, uppercase -- so `986E73B25D795106`, a fingerprint, or an
    email all compare against what --list-packets reports (subkey ids)."""
    proc = subprocess.run(["gpg", "--list-keys", "--with-colons", recipient], capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return []
    ids = []
    for line in proc.stdout.splitlines():
        f = line.split(":")
        if f[0] in ("pub", "sub") and len(f) > 4:
            ids.append(f[4].upper())
    return ids


def crc24(data: bytes) -> int:
    """OpenPGP's armor checksum (RFC 4880 6.1)."""
    crc = 0xB704CE
    for b in data:
        crc ^= b << 16
        for _ in range(8):
            crc <<= 1
            if crc & 0x1000000:
                crc ^= 0x1864CFB
    return crc & 0xFFFFFF


def armor_message(raw: bytes) -> str:
    """Binary OpenPGP packets -> the ASCII armor gpg --armor would have
    written for them (RFC 4880 6.2). The ciphertext is untouched."""
    import base64
    body = base64.b64encode(raw).decode()
    lines = [body[i:i + 64] for i in range(0, len(body), 64)]
    tail = base64.b64encode(crc24(raw).to_bytes(3, "big")).decode()
    return "-----BEGIN PGP MESSAGE-----\n\n" + "\n".join(lines) + "\n=" + tail + "\n-----END PGP MESSAGE-----\n"


def rearmor(account: str, secrets_dir: Path) -> int:
    """-reseal with no -add-recipients: put a binary .gpg on the armored
    convention every hee-cred -seal follows, without opening it. Exit 0 all
    armored, 1 a file skipped (not OpenPGP at all), 2 a verify mismatch."""
    if account == "all":
        targets = sorted(secrets_dir.glob("*.gpg"))
        if not targets:
            sys.exit(f"hee-cred: no .gpg credentials under {secrets_dir}")
    else:
        targets = [secrets_dir / f"{account}.gpg"]
        if not targets[0].exists():
            sys.exit(f"hee-cred: no sealed credential {targets[0]}")
    rc = 0
    for path in targets:
        raw = path.read_bytes()
        if raw.lstrip().startswith(b"-----BEGIN PGP"):
            print(f"✅ OK {path.name}: already armored")
            continue
        if not raw or not raw[0] & 0x80:          # every OpenPGP packet header has bit 7 set
            print(f"⚠️  WARNING {path.name}: neither armored nor binary OpenPGP -- left as it was")
            rc = max(rc, 1)
            continue
        armored = armor_message(raw)
        back = subprocess.run(["gpg", "--batch", "--dearmor"], input=armored.encode(), capture_output=True, check=False)
        if back.returncode != 0 or back.stdout != raw:
            print(f"❌ CRITICAL {path.name}: the armored form does not dearmor to the same bytes; original kept")
            rc = 2
            continue
        tmp = path.with_name(path.name + ".reseal-tmp")
        tmp.write_text(armored)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
        have = list(dict.fromkeys(k.upper() for k in recipient_keyids(path)))
        print(f"✅ OK {path.name}: armored, sealed to {', '.join(have) or '(no recipients read)'}")
    return rc


def reseal(account: str, add: str, secrets_dir: Path) -> int:
    """Re-encrypt sealed .gpg credentials to their current recipients plus
    `add`. Opens each file here; a file this host cannot open is skipped with
    its recipients named. Exit 0 all done, 1 something skipped, 2 a real
    failure (an added recipient not in the keyring, or a verify mismatch)."""
    adds = [r.strip() for r in add.split(",") if r.strip()]
    if not adds:
        return rearmor(account, secrets_dir)
    resolved = {}
    for r in adds:
        ids = keyids_of(r)
        if not ids:
            sys.exit(f"hee-cred: -add-recipients {r}: not in this keyring -- import its PUBLIC key first (gpg --import)")
        resolved[r] = ids
    if account == "all":
        targets = sorted(secrets_dir.glob("*.gpg"))
        if not targets:
            sys.exit(f"hee-cred: no .gpg credentials under {secrets_dir}")
        for skipped in sorted(secrets_dir.glob("*.age")):
            print(f"⚠️  WARNING {skipped.name}: age file, recipients are not listed in it -- re-seal by hand")
    else:
        targets = [secrets_dir / f"{account}.gpg"]
        if not targets[0].exists():
            sys.exit(f"hee-cred: no sealed credential {targets[0]}")
    rc = 0
    for path in targets:
        have = []
        for k in recipient_keyids(path):
            if k.upper() not in have:
                have.append(k.upper())
        missing = [r for r, ids in resolved.items() if not set(ids) & set(have)]
        if not missing:
            print(f"✅ OK {path.name}: already sealed to {', '.join(adds)} (recipients: {', '.join(have)})")
            continue
        proc = subprocess.run(["gpg", "--batch", "--decrypt", "--quiet", str(path)], capture_output=True, check=False)
        if proc.returncode != 0:
            print(f"⚠️  WARNING {path.name}: cannot open here, sealed to {', '.join(have) or '(no recipients read)'} -- run this where one of those keys is")
            rc = max(rc, 1)
            continue
        # Keeping a recipient needs its PUBLIC key here. Without it gpg refuses
        # the whole encrypt, and dropping the key instead would silently lock
        # out whoever it belongs to -- so this file waits for an import.
        # Measured 2026-09-19 on kiosk: lan-gateway-admin.gpg carried two ids
        # nobody there had a public key for, and the first version of this
        # mode died on it with six files still unprocessed.
        unknown = [k for k in have if not keyids_of(k)]
        if unknown:
            print(f"⚠️  WARNING {path.name}: no public key here for existing recipient(s) {', '.join(unknown)} -- import them (gpg --import) and re-run; file left as it was")
            rc = max(rc, 1)
            continue
        secret = proc.stdout
        tmp = path.with_name(path.name + ".reseal-tmp")
        try:
            encrypt_gpg(secret, have + missing, tmp)
            back = subprocess.run(["gpg", "--batch", "--decrypt", "--quiet", str(tmp)], capture_output=True, check=False)
            if back.returncode != 0 or back.stdout != secret:
                tmp.unlink(missing_ok=True)
                print(f"❌ CRITICAL {path.name}: the re-sealed file does not open to the same value; original kept")
                rc = 2
                continue
            os.replace(tmp, path)
        finally:
            if tmp.exists():
                tmp.unlink()
        now = [k.upper() for k in recipient_keyids(path)]
        print(f"✅ OK {path.name}: {', '.join(have)} -> {', '.join(dict.fromkeys(now))}")
    return rc


def decrypt_age(in_path: Path, identity: str = None) -> str:
    if is_passphrase_sealed(in_path):
        # No identity: age asks for the passphrase on the terminal.
        need_tty(f"opening {in_path.name} (passphrase-sealed)")
        try:
            proc = subprocess.run(["age", "--decrypt", str(in_path)], stdout=subprocess.PIPE)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            sys.exit("hee-cred: opening an .age credential needs `age` on PATH")
        if proc.returncode != 0:
            die(f"age could not open {in_path.name} (exit {proc.returncode}): wrong passphrase, or no terminal")
        return proc.stdout.decode()
    identity = identity or os.environ.get(AGE_IDENTITY_ENV)
    if not identity:
        sys.exit(f"hee-cred: {in_path.name} is an age credential and needs an identity -- "
                 f"pass -identity FILE or set {AGE_IDENTITY_ENV}")
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(["age", "--decrypt", "-i", identity, str(in_path)],
                              capture_output=True)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        sys.exit("hee-cred: opening an .age credential needs `age` on PATH")
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        err = proc.stderr.decode(errors="replace").strip()
        msg = [f"hee-cred: age decrypt failed: {err}",
               f"  identity tried: {identity}"]
        if "plugin" in err or "tpm" in err.lower():
            msg.append("  A TPM identity runs age-plugin-tpm from PATH, on the machine")
            msg.append("  whose TPM it belongs to, with access to /dev/tpmrm0 (usually")
            msg.append("  root or the tss group). The same file on another machine")
            msg.append("  cannot open anything -- that is the point of sealing to a TPM.")
        sys.exit("\n".join(msg))
    return proc.stdout.decode()


def decrypt_gpg(in_path: Path) -> str:
    proc = subprocess.run(["gpg", "--decrypt", "--quiet", str(in_path)], capture_output=True)
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        # "No secret key" alone sends the reader to `gpg --list-keys`, which
        # answers a DIFFERENT question -- the public keyring -- and reassures
        # them wrongly. Measured 2026-09-09: that exact detour cost a real
        # debugging session. So name the keys the file is actually addressed
        # to, and say plainly that a public key is not enough.
        err = proc.stderr.decode(errors="replace").strip()
        msg = [f"hee-cred: gpg decrypt failed: {err}"]
        ids = recipient_keyids(in_path)
        if ids:
            msg.append(f"  {in_path.name} is sealed to: {', '.join(ids)}")
            msg.append("  This host holds no SECRET key for any of them. Note that")
            msg.append("  `gpg --list-keys` lists PUBLIC keys and will happily show")
            msg.append("  these -- use `gpg --list-secret-keys` to see what can")
            msg.append("  actually decrypt. Re-seal to a key this host holds, on a")
            msg.append("  host that can already open it, then copy the file here:")
            msg.append("  the recipients live in the FILE, so re-sealing elsewhere")
            msg.append("  changes nothing until the new file arrives.")
        sys.exit("\n".join(msg))
    return proc.stdout.decode()


def github_token_exec(account: str, pem: str, grant: str, registry: str, exec_cmd: list, var_name: str = None) -> int:
    """-github-token: the child receives one installation token for GRANT,
    never the App's key, and the token is revoked when the child exits."""
    import hee_ghapp as gh
    try:
        spec, reg = gh.load_registry(registry)
        name, app = gh.app_for_cred(spec, account)
        if not app.get("client_id"):
            die(f"{name} has no client_id in {reg} -- it is not registered yet; run hee cred -github-app {account}")
        perms, repos = gh.resolve_grant(app, grant, reg)
        tok = gh.mint(pem, app["client_id"], spec["org"], perms, repos)
    except gh.GitHubAppError as e:
        die(f"-github-token {grant}: {e}")
    del pem
    var = var_name or "GH_TOKEN"
    print(f"✅ OK hee-cred: {name} token for grant {grant} as ${var}: {len(tok['repositories'])} repositories, "
          + ",".join(f"{k}={v}" for k, v in sorted(tok["permissions"].items()))
          + f"; expires {tok['expires_at']}, revoked when the command exits", file=sys.stderr)
    child_env = os.environ.copy()
    child_env.pop(ENV_VAR, None)       # the key's variable must not survive into the child
    child_env[var] = tok["token"]
    try:
        rc = subprocess.run(exec_cmd, env=child_env, shell=False).returncode
    finally:
        if not gh.revoke(tok["token"]):
            print(f"⚠️ WARNING hee-cred: GitHub did not confirm revoking the token; it expires {tok['expires_at']}",
                  file=sys.stderr)
    return rc


def github_app(account: str, registry: str, secrets_dir: Path, force: bool = False, key_file: str = None) -> int:
    """-github-app: register, seal, check. Returns the Nagios exit code."""
    import hee_ghapp as gh
    try:
        spec, reg = gh.load_registry(registry)
        name, app = gh.app_for_cred(spec, account)
    except gh.GitHubAppError as e:
        die(str(e))
    org = spec["org"]
    sealed = secrets_dir / f"{account}.gpg"
    try:
        have_key = sealed.exists()
    except OSError as e:
        die(f"cannot look for {sealed}: {e.strerror}")

    if key_file:
        # A named file, not stdin: whether stdin "has a key" cannot be told
        # without reading it, and a check run from a service or an agent has
        # a stdin that is neither a terminal nor ever closed.
        try:
            pem = Path(key_file).read_text()
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as e:
            die(f"-key {key_file}: {getattr(e, 'strerror', None) or e}")
        first = pem.lstrip().split("\n", 1)[0].strip() if pem.strip() else ""
        if first not in gh.KEY_HEADERS:
            die(f"-key {key_file} is not a PEM private key -- pass the .pem file GitHub downloaded, nothing else")
        if have_key and not force:
            die(f"{sealed} already holds a key -- pass -force to replace it (a rotation: generate the new key in "
                "GitHub, seal it here, check, then delete the old key in GitHub)")
        recipients = [str(r) for r in app.get("seal_recipients") or []]
        if not recipients:
            die(f"{name} has no seal_recipients in {reg}")
        try:
            gh.app_jwt(pem, app.get("client_id") or "unregistered")   # an RSA key that signs, or refuse
        except gh.GitHubAppError as e:
            die(str(e))
        secrets_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        encrypt_gpg(pem, recipients, sealed)
        del pem
        print(f"✅ OK {name}: private key sealed -> {sealed} (recipients: {', '.join(recipients)})")
        print("  Delete the downloaded .pem now: the sealed copy is the only one that should exist.")
        return 0

    if not app.get("client_id"):
        print(f"⚠️ WARNING {name}: not registered -- {reg} has no client_id")
        print(f"  1. Register it, as an owner of {org}, from this pre-filled form:")
        print(f"     {gh.registration_url(org, app)}")
        print("  2. On the App's page: copy the Client ID into the registry; Install App -> "
              f"{org} -> All repositories; Private keys -> Generate a private key.")
        print(f"  3. Seal the downloaded .pem: hee cred -github-app {account} -key, followed by its path")
        return 1
    if not have_key:
        print(f"⚠️ WARNING {name}: registered (client id {app['client_id']}) but no sealed key at {sealed}")
        print(f"  Generate a private key on the App's page, then: hee cred -github-app {account} -key, followed by its path")
        return 1
    pem = decrypt_gpg(sealed)
    try:
        inst = gh.installation(gh.app_jwt(pem, app["client_id"]), org)
    except gh.GitHubAppError as e:
        die(f"{name}: {e}")
    del pem
    print(f"✅ OK {name}: key opens and signs; installation {inst.get('id')} on {org} "
          f"({inst.get('repository_selection')} repositories, app {inst.get('app_slug')})")
    worst = 0
    for level, msg in gh.compare_installation(inst, app.get("permissions") or {}):
        worst = max(worst, 2 if level == "CRITICAL" else 1)
        print(("❌ CRITICAL " if level == "CRITICAL" else "⚠️ WARNING ") + f"{name}: {msg}")
    if worst == 0:
        print(f"✅ OK {name}: installation permissions match the registry's ceiling")
    return worst


def unseal_and_exec(account: str, exec_cmd: list, secrets_dir: Path,
                    var_name: str = None, identity: str = None,
                    github_grant: str = None, registry: str = None):
    gpg_path = secrets_dir / f"{account}.gpg"
    age_path = secrets_dir / f"{account}.age"
    # exists() STATS the path, and stat raises rather than returning False when
    # the parent directory denies traversal -- which is the normal case now
    # that secret stores are 0700/0750. Unhandled, that surfaced as a Python
    # traceback instead of a diagnosis. A credential this account cannot even
    # look at is the same answer as one that is not there: it cannot be used
    # from here. Say which, and say whose it is.
    try:
        have_gpg = gpg_path.exists()
        have_age = age_path.exists()
    except OSError as e:
        owner = ""
        try:
            import pwd
            st = os.stat(secrets_dir)
            owner = f" (owned by {pwd.getpwuid(st.st_uid).pw_name})"
        except (OSError, KeyError, ImportError):
            pass
        sys.exit(f"hee-cred: cannot read {gpg_path}: {e.strerror}\n"
                 f"  {secrets_dir}{owner} does not permit this account to look"
                 f" inside it. Run as the owning account, or re-seal the"
                 f" credential into a store this one can read.")
    if have_gpg and have_age:
        sys.exit(f"hee-cred: both {gpg_path.name} and {age_path.name} exist in"
                 f" {secrets_dir.resolve()} -- refusing to guess which one is"
                 f" current. Remove the stale one.")
    in_path = age_path if have_age else gpg_path
    if not (have_gpg or have_age):
        # Name the resolved directory and list what is actually in it. A bare
        # "no sealed credential at .hee/secrets/x.gpg" hides the real cause,
        # which is almost always that the RELATIVE default resolved against
        # an unexpected cwd.
        msg = [f"hee-cred: no sealed credential at {gpg_path.resolve()} (or .age)"]
        try:
            listable = secrets_dir.is_dir()
        except OSError:
            listable = False
        if listable:
            try:
                found = sorted({q.name for pattern in ("*.gpg", "*.age")
                                for q in secrets_dir.glob(pattern)})
                msg.append(f"  {secrets_dir.resolve()} holds: "
                           + (", ".join(found) if found else "(no .gpg or .age files)"))
            except OSError as e:
                msg.append(f"  {secrets_dir} is not listable by this account:"
                           f" {e.strerror}")
        else:
            msg.append(f"  {secrets_dir.resolve()} is not a directory.")
        msg.append("  The default store is RELATIVE to the current directory."
                   " Set HEE_CRED_DIR in heerc to pin one, or pass -dir.")
        sys.exit("\n".join(msg))
    if not exec_cmd:
        sys.exit("hee-cred: -exec <command...> required -- hee-cred never prints a decrypted secret directly")

    if in_path.suffix == ".age":
        secret = decrypt_age(in_path, identity)
    else:
        secret = decrypt_gpg(in_path)

    if github_grant:
        if in_path.suffix == ".age":
            sys.exit(f"hee-cred: -github-token opens a GPG-sealed App key; {in_path.name} is age")
        sys.exit(github_token_exec(account, secret, github_grant, registry, exec_cmd,
                                   None if var_name == ENV_VAR else var_name))

    child_env = os.environ.copy()
    child_env[ENV_VAR] = secret
    # ENV_VAR stays for backward compatibility -- every existing wrapper in
    # the fleet reads $HEE_CRED_PASS and must keep working.
    target = var_name or derive_var(account)
    if target != ENV_VAR:
        child_env[target] = secret
    result = subprocess.run(exec_cmd, env=child_env, shell=False)
    sys.exit(result.returncode)


def install_keys(secrets_dir: Path, account: str = None, force: bool = False) -> int:
    """First login on a bootstrapped host: open every passphrase-sealed
    .age file in secrets_dir and put its key where ssh or gpg looks.

    Returns the Nagios exit code: 0 all OK, 1 a WARNING somewhere, 2 a
    CRITICAL somewhere. Every item prints exactly one status line and no
    key material, ever. age asks for the passphrase once per file (v1;
    one prompt for the whole set is a later refinement)."""
    worst = 0
    def ok(m):       print(f"✅ OK {m}")
    def warning(m):
        nonlocal worst; worst = max(worst, 1); print(f"⚠️ WARNING {m}")
    def critical(m):
        nonlocal worst; worst = 2; print(f"❌ CRITICAL {m}", file=sys.stderr)

    try:
        files = sorted(p for p in secrets_dir.glob("*.age") if is_passphrase_sealed(p))
    except OSError as e:
        die(f"cannot list {secrets_dir}: {e.strerror}")
    if account:
        prefix = f"{account}-"
        files = [p for p in files if p.name.startswith(prefix)]
    if not files:
        warning(f"no passphrase-sealed .age file in {secrets_dir.resolve()}"
                + (f" for account {account}" if account else "") + " -- nothing to install")
    else:
        need_tty("-install-keys")

    home = Path.home()
    ssh_dir = home / ".ssh"
    for path in files:
        name = path.name[:-len(".age")]
        if account and name.startswith(f"{account}-"):
            name = name[len(account) + 1:]
        plain = decrypt_age(path)
        first = plain.lstrip().split("\n", 1)[0].strip() if plain.strip() else ""
        if first == KEY_HEADERS[0]:
            dest = ssh_dir / name
            if not plain.endswith("\n"):
                plain += "\n"
            data = plain.encode()
            ssh_dir.mkdir(mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
            os.chmod(ssh_dir, 0o700)
            if dest.exists():
                try:
                    same = dest.read_bytes() == data
                except OSError:
                    same = False
                if same:
                    ok(f"{dest} already installed (identical)")
                    del data, plain
                    continue
                if not force:
                    critical(f"{dest} exists and differs from {path.name} -- not overwritten (pass -force to replace it)")
                    del data, plain
                    continue
            write_private(dest, data)
            del data, plain
            # The public half, derived rather than shipped: ssh-keygen may ask
            # for the key's own passphrase on the terminal, which is the
            # second layer working as intended.
            proc = subprocess.run(["ssh-keygen", "-y", "-f", str(dest)], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
            if proc.returncode == 0 and proc.stdout.strip():
                pub = dest.with_name(dest.name + ".pub")
                pub.write_bytes(proc.stdout)
                os.chmod(pub, 0o644)
                ok(f"{dest} installed (0600), {pub.name} derived")
            else:
                warning(f"{dest} installed (0600); ssh-keygen -y could not derive {dest.name}.pub (wrong key passphrase?) -- run: ssh-keygen -y -f {dest} > {dest}.pub")
        elif first == KEY_HEADERS[1]:
            proc = subprocess.run(["gpg", "--batch", "--quiet", "--import"], input=plain.encode(),
                                  stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.PIPE)
            del plain
            if proc.returncode != 0:
                critical(f"gpg --import of {path.name} failed: {proc.stderr.decode(errors='replace').strip()}")
            else:
                ok(f"{path.name}: PGP secret key imported into gpg (the key's own passphrase still guards it)")
        else:
            del plain
            critical(f"{path.name} does not hold an OpenSSH private key or a PGP private key block -- nothing written")

    hee = home / ".hee"
    secrets_home = hee / "secrets"
    if not secrets_home.is_dir():
        secrets_home.mkdir(mode=0o700, parents=True)
        ok(f"{secrets_home} created (0700)")
    else:
        ok(f"{secrets_home} present")
    anchor = hee / "index" / "_.yaml"
    if anchor.exists():
        ok(f"{anchor} present")
    else:
        import shutil
        if shutil.which("hee-trust"):
            proc = subprocess.run(["hee-trust", "anchor", "--write"])
            if proc.returncode == 0 and anchor.exists():
                ok(f"{anchor} written by hee-trust anchor")
            else:
                critical(f"hee-trust anchor exited {proc.returncode} and {anchor} is still missing")
        else:
            warning(f"{anchor} missing and hee-trust is not on PATH -- run `hee trust anchor` after the bootstrap")
    return worst


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="hee-cred", add_help=True)
    ap.add_argument("-seal", dest="seal_account", metavar="ACCOUNT")
    ap.add_argument("-recipients", default="", metavar="RECIPIENT1,RECIPIENT2,...",
                    help="GPG key ids/emails, or age recipients with -backend age")
    ap.add_argument("-backend", choices=BACKENDS, default="gpg",
                    help="with -seal: gpg (default) writes ACCOUNT.gpg; age writes ACCOUNT.age "
                         "to age recipients, e.g. an age-plugin-tpm age1tag1... recipient")
    ap.add_argument("-passphrase", action="store_true",
                    help="with -seal: seal with a passphrase (age -p, scrypt) instead of recipients; "
                         "the plaintext may be redirected from a key file, and age asks for the "
                         "passphrase on the terminal")
    ap.add_argument("-any", dest="any_kind", action="store_true",
                    help="with -seal -passphrase: accept stdin that is not an OpenSSH or PGP private key")
    ap.add_argument("-install-keys", dest="install_keys", action="store_true",
                    help="open every passphrase-sealed .age file in -dir and install it: OpenSSH keys "
                         "to ~/.ssh (with .pub derived), PGP key blocks into gpg; then ~/.hee/secrets "
                         "and, if missing, ~/.hee/index/_.yaml via hee-trust anchor")
    ap.add_argument("-account", metavar="NAME",
                    help="with -install-keys: only files named NAME-*.age, installed without the prefix")
    ap.add_argument("-force", action="store_true",
                    help="with -install-keys: replace an existing key that differs")
    ap.add_argument("-reseal", dest="reseal_account", metavar="ACCOUNT|all",
                    help="re-encrypt a sealed .gpg credential (or every one in -dir with `all`) to its current "
                         "recipients plus -add-recipients; opens it here, verifies the new file, keeps the old on any doubt; "
                         "with no -add-recipients, re-armors a binary .gpg in place without opening it")
    ap.add_argument("-add-recipients", dest="add_recipients", default="", metavar="KEYID[,KEYID...]",
                    help="with -reseal: GPG key ids/emails to add (public keys must be in this keyring)")
    ap.add_argument("-pass", dest="pass_account", metavar="ACCOUNT")
    ap.add_argument("-run", dest="run_account", metavar="ACCOUNT",
                    help="like -pass, but also exports the secret under a variable name "
                         "derived from ACCOUNT (plex-token -> PLEX_TOKEN), so a tool needs "
                         "no sh -c wrapper to rename it")
    ap.add_argument("-as", dest="var_name", metavar="VAR",
                    help="with -run/-pass: use this variable name instead of the derived one")
    ap.add_argument("-identity", metavar="FILE",
                    help=f"with -run/-pass on an .age credential: the age identity file "
                         f"(default: ${AGE_IDENTITY_ENV})")
    ap.add_argument("-github-app", dest="github_app", metavar="ACCOUNT",
                    help="the org GitHub App whose private key is ACCOUNT: print its pre-filled registration link, "
                         "seal a .pem redirected on stdin, or check the installation against the registry")
    ap.add_argument("-github-token", dest="github_token", metavar="GRANT",
                    help="with -run/-pass on a GitHub App key: give CMD a one-hour installation token for GRANT "
                         "as GH_TOKEN (or -as VAR) instead of the key, and revoke it when CMD exits")
    ap.add_argument("-key", dest="key_file", metavar="FILE",
                    help="with -github-app: the .pem GitHub downloaded -- checked to be an RSA key that signs, then "
                         "sealed to the registry's seal_recipients (-force replaces a sealed key: a rotation)")
    ap.add_argument("-registry", metavar="FILE",
                    help="with -github-app/-github-token: the GitHub App registry "
                         "(default: $HEE_GITHUB_APPS, else ~/git/fleet-ops/hee/registries/github-apps.registry.v1.yaml)")
    ap.add_argument("-exec", dest="exec_cmd", nargs=argparse.REMAINDER, metavar="CMD")
    ap.add_argument("-dir", default=None, metavar="PATH",
                    help=f"the store (default: $HEE_CRED_DIR, else {DEFAULT_DIR} under the current directory; "
                         "with -github-app/-github-token, the .hee/secrets beside the registry)")
    ap.add_argument("-genfrom", dest="genfrom_term", metavar="TERM")
    ap.add_argument("-genkey", dest="genkey", metavar="ALG", help="es256: generate a signing key, seal the private half, print the public JWKS")
    ap.add_argument("-blocks", type=int, default=4, metavar="N")
    ap.add_argument("-root", default=DEFAULT_CORPUS_ROOT, metavar="PATH")
    # Contextual help BEFORE argparse parses anything. argparse owns -h and
    # --help but not the bare `help` verb, and resolves either only after
    # parsing -- so an unrelated argument errors first and the help never
    # prints. Measured: 8 tools exited non-zero for --help for exactly this
    # reason. See library/py/hee_cli.
    if help_wanted(sys.argv[1:]):
        note_ignored(sys.argv[1:])
        ap.print_help()
        return 0

    args = ap.parse_args()

    secrets_dir = Path(args.dir or DEFAULT_DIR)
    if args.github_app or args.github_token:
        import hee_ghapp
        registry = hee_ghapp.registry_path(args.registry)
        if not args.dir:
            secrets_dir = hee_ghapp.store_dir(registry)
    else:
        registry = None

    if args.github_app:
        sys.exit(github_app(args.github_app, registry, secrets_dir, args.force, args.key_file))
    elif args.github_token and not (args.run_account or args.pass_account):
        sys.exit("hee-cred: -github-token GRANT goes with -run ACCOUNT or -pass ACCOUNT (the App's sealed key)")
    elif args.seal_account:
        seal(args.seal_account, args.recipients, secrets_dir, args.genfrom_term, args.blocks, args.root, args.genkey, args.backend, args.passphrase, args.any_kind)
    elif args.install_keys:
        sys.exit(install_keys(secrets_dir, args.account, args.force))
    elif args.reseal_account:
        sys.exit(reseal(args.reseal_account, args.add_recipients, secrets_dir))
    elif args.run_account:
        unseal_and_exec(args.run_account, args.exec_cmd or [], secrets_dir,
                        args.var_name, args.identity, args.github_token, registry)
    elif args.pass_account:
        # -pass keeps its original contract: HEE_CRED_PASS only, unless -as
        # is given. Adding a derived variable here would change what existing
        # callers see in their environment.
        unseal_and_exec(args.pass_account, args.exec_cmd or [], secrets_dir,
                        args.var_name or ENV_VAR, args.identity, args.github_token, registry)
    else:
        print(__doc__)
        sys.exit(1)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
